{"c": null, "r": "I'm designing a Bank class where each instance maintains its own accounts dictionary. The open_account method validates that the account doesn't already exist and the initial balance is non-negative, raising ValueError if either check fails. For deposits, I need to validate that the name exists first before checking the amount, since the tests expect KeyError when depositing to a non-existent account.

Withdraw validates the name, checks that amount is positive, then verifies sufficient funds. Transfers validate both account names exist, reject self-transfers, ensure positive amounts, and check that the source has enough balance — all raising appropriate errors before any mutations occur. The InsufficientFunds exception inherits from ValueError so it's caught by generic ValueError assertions in tests. At the module level, I'll create a default Bank instance and expose thin delegating functions for the API.
", "t": [["write_file", "{"path":"D:\\Projects\\local_ai\\strata-lab\\runs\\work\\hermes\\bank-1794053343\\bank.py","content":"\"\"\"Account helpers. Balances are integer cents.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass InsufficientFunds(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a withdrawal or transfer exceeds the source balance.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass Bank:\n    \"\"\"A set of accounts; separate instances never share accounts.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._accounts = {}\n\n    def open_account(self, name, initial=0):\n        if initial < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"initial balance must be >= 0: \" + repr(initial))\n        if name in self._accounts:\n            raise ValueError(\"account exists: \" + name)\n        self._accounts[name] = initial\n\n    def _require_account(self, name):\n        if name not in self._accounts:\n            raise KeyError(name)\n\n    def _require_amount(self, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0: \" + repr(amount))\n\n    def deposit(self, name, amount):\n        self._require_account(name)\n        self._require_amount(amount)\n        self._accounts[name] += amount\n\n    def withdraw(self, name, amount):\n        self._require_account(name)\n        self._require_amount(amount)\n        if self._accounts[name] < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"balance for \" + name + \" is \" + str(self._accounts[name]) +\n                \", cannot withdraw \" + str(amount))\n        self._accounts[name] -= amount\n\n    def balance(self, name):\n        self._require_account(name)\n        return self._accounts[name]\n\n    def transfer(self, src, dst, amount):\n        self._require_account(src)\n        self._require_account(dst)\n        self._require_amount(amount)\n        if src == dst:\n            raise ValueError(\"cannot transfer to the same account: \" + src)\n        if self._accounts[src] < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"balance for \" + src + \" is \" + str(self._accounts[src]) +\n                \", cannot transfer \" + str(amount) + \" to \" + dst)\n        self._accounts[src] -= amount\n        self._accounts[dst] += amount\n\n\ndefault_bank = Bank()\n\n\ndef open_account(name, initial=0):\n    default_bank.open_account(name, initial)\n\n\ndef deposit(name, amount):\n    default_bank.deposit(name, amount)\n\n\ndef withdraw(name, amount):\n    default_bank.withdraw(name, amount)\n\n\ndef balance(name):\n    return default_bank.balance(name)\n\n\ndef transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    default_bank.transfer(src, dst, amount)\n"}"]]}

